Our teams have been busy! Here are some key updates from the past week: 

@GoogleCloud unveiled a suite of AI innovations at our Cloud Next event, including our eighth generation TPUs (TPUt for inference and TPUi for reasoning), Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, Agentic Data cloud, Workspace Intelligence, and more. Gemini Embedding 2, our natively multimodal embedding model, became generally available via the Gemini API and in the Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, which is the evolution of Vertex AI.

@StitchbyGoogle open-sourced the draft specification for DESIGN.md, allowing it to be used across any single tool or platform. New autonomous search agents, Deep Research and Deep Research Max, launched to bring MCP support, native visualizations, and unprecedented analytical quality to research workflows across the web or custom sources. Google AI Pro and Ultra subscribers now enjoy increased usage limits and access to Nano Banana Pro and Gemini Pro models in @GoogleAIStudio. Finally, @GoogleDeepMind introduced Decoupled DiLoCo, a new resilient and flexible way to train advanced AI models across multiple data centers.

Great work in advancing the resilience and scalability of AI training infrastructure. Decoupled DiLoCo represents a significant step in long-term research into distributed training. By removing the requirement that model replicas must be updated in lockstep, this approach moves toward training that is no longer bottlenecked by a single straggling chip or limited to a single location.

This work builds on the communication reduction benefits of earlier DiLoCo studies. Decoupled DiLoCo introduces techniques for failure resilience to achieve high goodput and near 100% uptime, even when training across heterogeneous hardware like TPU v6e and v5p.

@Ar_Douillard: "The DiLoCo team at Google DeepMind and Google Research is proud to release Decoupled DiLoCo, the next frontier for resilient AI pre-training. Decoupled DiLoCo enables training with datacenters across the world, using heterogeneous hardware, and never halting the system despite hardware failures."

